The file has one declaration. I found no defects in its mathematics.

Primary text (Erdős–Graham 1980, p. 29), which I decoded myself from the scanned PDF: "More generally, one could define (a_i (mod n_i)) to be a covering system if for some k, all but a finite number of positive integers are of the form a_i + t n_i for some t ≥ k. Is it true that with this definition, the primes form the moduli of an infinite covering system for every k? Even the case k = 3 already seems to be difficult." The Lean statement matches this and the current erdosproblems wording:
- quantifier order ∀k ∃a ∃N ∀n ∃p ∃t;
- t ≥ k;
- all sufficiently large n;
- one residue class per prime;
- answer polarity not negated.

Sanity checks in /workspace/review (all compiled, no errors, no sorry in my lemmas):
- the k = 1 instance holds trivially with a_p = 0;
- with a_p = 0 and k ≥ 2 no prime is covered, so the statement is not trivially true;
- monotonicity in k;
- equivalence with unconstrained ℕ-representatives;
- equivalence with representatives in [1, p] under ∀k;
- triviality with unrestricted ℤ-representatives.

Status: erdosproblems.com (9 Jul 2026 snapshot) says OPEN, 'cannot be resolved with a finite computation'. The forum has no solution claims; the only proof attempt in the note, its 'Theorem 7', was found to have a gap. The teorth database also lists the problem as open. So the file's `research open` tag is current.

On sources: I could not load the live erdosproblems.com page because of Cloudflare, so I relied on Wayback snapshots and the live teorth database. The remark on the page was changed on 17 Apr 2026 (to the 'almost all integers' version); the file does not formalize it.

A documentation point, not a defect: the module docstring cites only erdosproblems.com, not the original [ErGr80, p. 29].
